## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to build a multi-tenant TLS termination with live updates in a containerized RH9 setup, including security and networking constraints.

## Key Concepts
- TLS with SNI and per-tenant certs
- Podman container networking
- Local CA management and cert rotation
- systemd-based zero-downtime reloads
- SELinux policy and nftables isolation
- End-to-end test planning

## Code Example
```javascript
// watcher: rotate tenant certs and reload proxy
const fs = require('fs');
const { execSync } = require('child_process');
const watchDir = '/var/lib/proxy/certs';
const watcher = require('chokidar').watch(watchDir);
watcher.on('change', (path) => {
  if (path.endsWith('.pem')) {
    fs.copyFileSync(`${watchDir}/current.pem`, '/var/run/proxy/current.pem.bak');
    execSync('systemctl reload rhproxy');
  }
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you revoke a tenant cert without downtime?
- How would you validate multi-tenant isolation under load?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a containerized TLS gateway with per-tenant isolation and real-time traffic policing, plus zero-downtime upgrades and firewall hardening. It also probes integration of Podman, Nginx, SNI routing, mTLS to backends, and practical rollback plans.

## Key Concepts
- Podman and Nginx TLS termination with SNI routing
- mTLS backends and backend certificate verification
- Per-tenant rate limiting (limit_req_zone or Lua scripting)
- Dynamic config reloads via systemd drop-in for zero-downtime upgrades
- nftables rules for inbound 8443 and outbound to backends
- Concrete test plans for bursts and rollback

## Code Example
```nginx
# nginx.conf (excerpt)
http {
  limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=tenantA:10m rate=10r/s;
  limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=tenantB:10m rate=5r/s;
  server {
    listen 8443 ssl;
    server_name tenantA.example;
    ssl_certificate /path/a.crt;
    ssl_certificate_key /path/a.key;
    location / {
      limit_req zone=tenantA burst=100 nodelay;
      proxy_pass https://10.0.2.50:8080;
      proxy_ssl_server_name on;
      proxy_ssl_verify on;
    }
  }
  server {
    listen 8443 ssl;
    server_name tenantB.example;
    location / {
      limit_req zone=tenantB burst=50;
      proxy_pass https://10.0.2.51:8080;
      proxy_ssl_server_name on;
      proxy_ssl_verify on;
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale tenants beyond two while preserving fairness? 
- What changes for a third backend or different TLS ciphers? 
- How would you monitor and alert on rate-limit breaches and reload status?

## Why This Is Asked
This scenario tests practical container containment, SELinux policy implementation, audit trail configuration, and host isolation in a realistic data ingestion workflow.

## Key Concepts
- Podman user namespaces for UID/GID isolation
- SELinux targeted policy modules for domain confinement
- Auditd path monitoring for comprehensive file I/O tracking
- Secure bind mounts with minimal host filesystem exposure

## Code Example
```bash
# Create SELinux module
module ingest_t 1.0;

require {
    type unconfined_t;
    type var_run_t;
    type etc_t;
    type default_t;
    class file { read write open getattr };
    class dir { search read open getattr };
    class sock_file { write open };
}

# Policy rules for ingest_t domain
allow ingest_t etc_t:file { read open getattr };
allow ingest_t var_run_t:sock_file { write open };
allow ingest_t default_t:dir { search read open getattr };
allow ingest_t default_t:file { read write open getattr };
```

## Implementation Commands
```bash
# Create dedicated user
sudo useradd -r -s /bin/false ingest
sudo usermod -v 100000-199999 -w 100000-199999 ingest

# Build and load SELinux module
cat > ingest.te << 'EOF'
module ingest_t 1.0;

require {
    type unconfined_t;
    type var_run_t;
    type etc_t;
    type default_t;
    class file { read write open getattr };
    class dir { search read open getattr };
    class sock_file { write open };
}

allow ingest_t etc_t:file { read open getattr };
allow ingest_t var_run_t:sock_file { write open };
allow ingest_t default_t:dir { search read open getattr };
allow ingest_t default_t:file { read write open getattr };
EOF

checkmodule -M -m -o ingest.mod ingest.te
semodule_package -o ingest.pp -m ingest.mod
sudo semodule -i ingest.pp

# Configure audit rules
sudo auditctl -w /opt/ingest/config -p r -k ingest_config
sudo auditctl -w /srv/ingest/data -p rwxa -k ingest_data
sudo auditctl -w /var/run/ingest-proxy.sock -p w -k ingest_ipc

# Run container
podman run \
  --uidmap 0:100000:65536 \
  --gidmap 0:100000:65536 \
  -v /opt/ingest/config:/opt/ingest/config:ro \
  -v /srv/ingest/data:/srv/ingest/data:rw \
  -v /var/run/ingest-proxy.sock:/var/run/ingest-proxy.sock \
  --network=none \
  --security-opt label=type:ingest_t \
  --user 1000 \
  ingest-service:latest
```
